      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-slate-800 mb-2">7,235</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">BC PNP Skills Immigration registrations on November 3, 2026</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <nav class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">In this roundup</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-1 text-blue-700">
        <li><a href="#express-entry" class="hover:underline">Express Entry: why there have been no rounds</a></li>
        <li><a href="#recalculation" class="hover:underline">What the recalculation changes in your score</a></li>
        <li><a href="#bc-pnp" class="hover:underline">BC PNP: still waiting for the next draw</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rural" class="hover:underline">November 9: rural immigration becomes permanent</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tfwp" class="hover:underline">November 6: low-wage LMIA changes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-to-do" class="hover:underline">What to do this week</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:underline">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        This has been a quiet week for invitations and a busy one for policy. Candidates who usually watch for a mid-month Canadian Experience Class round or a BC PNP Care and Build draw have had nothing new to react to, while the federal government finalized changes that affect who ranks highest once rounds return. This roundup sticks to what the official sources show as of November 12, 2026. It is general information, not legal advice.
      </p>

      <h2 id="express-entry" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Express Entry: Why There Have Been No Rounds Since October 28</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC announced on <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/news/2026/10/canada-refocusing-express-entry-on-top-talent-that-drives-economic-growth.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">October 16, 2026</a> that from November 3 it would award new Comprehensive Ranking System (CRS) points for eligible Canadian work experience or a qualifying job offer in a high-wage occupation. Profiles already in the pool would be recalculated automatically, and the release said the process "may take up to two weeks," with invitation rounds paused in the meantime. IRCC's <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/immigrate-canada/express-entry/changes.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Express Entry changes page</a> repeats that rounds will "start again when it's complete" and gives no date.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before the pause, IRCC cleared a run of rounds in late October. The last six, from IRCC's official rounds data:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 text-base">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left">Round</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left">Date</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left">Type</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-right">Invitations</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-right">CRS cutoff</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">453</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Oct 28, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Transport occupations</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">150</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">477</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">452</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Oct 27, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-2">French-language proficiency</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">5,000</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">407</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">451</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Oct 26, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Provincial Nominee Program</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">792</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">761</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">450</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Oct 16, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-2">French-language proficiency</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">5,000</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">435</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">449</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Oct 15, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Canadian Experience Class</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">2,500</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">519</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">448</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Oct 13, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-2">Provincial Nominee Program</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">752</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">772</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        That is 5,942 invitations in the three days before the pause, and 14,194 across the six October rounds shown. The pool snapshot attached to round 453 counted 228,724 profiles as of October 25, 2026. As of November 12, no round 454 appears on the rounds page. Two weeks from November 3 would be November 17, but treat that as the end of IRCC's estimate, not a promised draw date.
      </p>

      <h2 id="recalculation" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What the Recalculation Changes in Your Score</h2>

      <p>
        The pause exists because scores are moving, so the first rounds after it may not look like October's. According to IRCC's changes page:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>One new factor, not two.</strong> Canadian work experience and qualifying job offers now sit under a Labour Market Integration factor worth up to 150 points. You get points from one or the other, not both.</li>
        <li><strong>Canadian experience.</strong> The maximum is 150 points without a spouse or partner (80 for length of work plus 70 for high-wage work) and 140 with one (70 plus 70).</li>
        <li><strong>Job offers.</strong> A qualifying offer in an eligible high-wage occupation can earn up to 100 points. An occupation qualifies if its national median wage is at least 1.3 times the average median wage, based on the NOC code rather than your own salary.</li>
        <li><strong>No LMIA needed.</strong> Since November 3, a job offer does not need a Labour Market Impact Assessment to count for eligibility or points, and having one adds nothing. Officers still check that the offer is genuine at the PR stage.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For B.C. workers, the practical effect is that candidates with Canadian experience in high-wage occupations may move up, while profiles that relied on LMIA-backed offers in lower-wage jobs may move down. Nobody outside IRCC knows the new score distribution yet, so be cautious with any cutoff "prediction" you see this week. Our guide on <a href="/articles/how-to-become-permanent-resident-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how to become a permanent resident in Canada</a> explains the November 3 changes in more depth.
      </p>
      <p>
        Provincial nominees are the group least affected. A nomination adds 600 points, which is why the October PNP rounds cut off at 761 and 772. If you hold a BC PNP Express Entry nomination, your profile should simply wait for the next PNP round; see our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-express-entry-bc-eebc-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry BC guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bc-pnp" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">BC PNP: Still Waiting for the Next Draw</h2>

      <p>
        WelcomeBC's <a href="https://www.welcomebc.ca/Immigrate-to-B-C/Invitations-To-Apply" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">Invitations to Apply page</a> was last updated November 3, 2026, and shows no November draw. The most recent Skills Immigration rounds were the October 8 Care and Build draw, a small Temporary Rural/Remote Health Support Initiative draw on October 15 (16 invitations, minimum score 54) and the October 29 Innovate draw. The Innovate wage threshold has dropped in each of the last three rounds:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 text-base">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left">Innovate draw</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-left">Wage threshold</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-right">Wage invitations</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-right">Points cutoff</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2 text-right">Points invitations</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">Oct 29, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$49/hour, $99,000/year</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">524</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">131</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">248</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">Sep 24, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$52/hour, $105,000/year</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">426</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">131</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">288</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t"><td class="px-4 py-2">Aug 20, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-2">$55/hour, $110,000/year</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">337</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">132</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-right">265</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        On October 8, the Care and Build cutoffs were 99 for childcare (86 invitations), 76 for health (82), 86 for veterinary care (fewer than 5) and 94 for construction trades (115). The November 3 pool snapshot counted 7,235 registrations, with only 119 at 130 points or more, so a points-based Innovate cutoff in the low 130s now draws from a small group.
      </p>
      <p>
        The bigger signal is the calendar. B.C.'s 2026 allocation is 6,254 nominations after the federal government added 1,000 on August 18, 2026, and WelcomeBC said the program "plans to use its full allocation by December 31, 2026." That suggests more invitations before year-end, but WelcomeBC does not publish a draw schedule, so no date is confirmed. Track results on our <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP draws page</a>, check your registration score with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a>, and see our analysis of <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-innovate-high-economic-impact-draws-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Innovate High Economic Impact draws</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="rural" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">November 9: Rural Immigration Becomes Permanent</h2>

      <p>
        In a <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/news/2026/11/canada-creates-a-permanent-immigration-pathway-to-help-rural-communities-attract-and-retain-talent0.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">November 9, 2026 news release</a>, IRCC announced that the Rural Community Immigration Pilot, running since 2025, will become a permanent, employer- and community-led Rural Community Immigration Program. It will continue with the current 14 communities, with more to be added later under criteria still to be announced, and the 2027–2029 Immigration Levels Plan targets 5,500 admissions for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        For B.C. readers, the <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/news/2026/11/overview-of-the-rural-community-immigration-program.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">IRCC backgrounder</a> lists three participating B.C. communities: <strong>West Kootenay</strong>, <strong>North Okanagan Shuswap</strong> and <strong>Peace Liard</strong>. If you work for a designated employer in one of them, the rural program is a federal PR option that runs alongside the BC PNP, and a regional job also earns extra BC PNP area points. Our <a href="/articles/rcip-fcip-rural-francophone-immigration-pilots-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">RCIP and FCIP guide</a> covers eligibility and community recommendations.
      </p>
      <p>
        Separately, IRCC's levels plan includes a time-limited measure to move people already in Canada to PR in 2027 and 2028. We cover those numbers in our <a href="/articles/canada-immigration-levels-plan-2027-2029-what-to-expect" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">2027–2029 levels plan article</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="tfwp" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">November 6: Low-Wage LMIA Changes</h2>

      <p>
        Employment and Social Development Canada (ESDC) announced <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/employment-social-development/news/2026/11/government-of-canada-announces-targeted-reforms-to-the-temporary-foreign-worker-program-to-respond-to-labour-market-needs.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">targeted reforms to the Temporary Foreign Worker Program on November 6, 2026</a>:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Longer low-wage LMIAs.</strong> Low-wage stream applications submitted on or after November 6, 2026 can cover up to two years of employment, up from one.</li>
        <li><strong>Early childhood educators exempt.</strong> ECE jobs join health care, construction and food processing as exceptions to the rule that ESDC will not process low-wage LMIAs in census metropolitan areas with unemployment of 6% or more.</li>
        <li><strong>Optional changes from March 1, 2027.</strong> If a province or territory opts in, employers in communities it identifies could fill up to 15% of their workforce through the low-wage stream, up from 10%. Priority sectors keep their 20% limit.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        ESDC's <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/employment-social-development/services/foreign-workers/refusal.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 underline">refusal-to-process list</a> for applications submitted from October 9, 2026 to January 7, 2027 includes four B.C. areas: Kelowna (8.6%), Abbotsford-Mission (7.6%), Vancouver (7.0%) and Nanaimo (6.6%). Victoria (5.8%), Chilliwack (5.6%) and Kamloops (3.6%) are below the line. Because most BC PNP Skills Immigration candidates first need a valid work permit, this matters for people planning their route. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-lmia-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP LMIA guide</a> explains how the two systems connect.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-to-do" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What to Do This Week</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Express Entry candidates:</strong> log in and confirm that your Canadian work history, NOC codes and any job offer are accurate. Do not create a duplicate profile to "refresh" your score. Recalculation is automatic.</li>
        <li><strong>Profile expiry:</strong> if your profile expires during the pause, IRCC's usual rules apply. Check your expiry date and be ready to create a new profile if needed.</li>
        <li><strong>BC PNP registrants:</strong> keep your registration current. A pay rise, a new language test or a move to a job outside Metro Vancouver can change your score before the next draw.</li>
        <li><strong>Work permit holders:</strong> if your employer plans a low-wage LMIA, ask whether your job is in a refusal-to-process area or an exempt occupation before you rely on it.</li>
        <li><strong>Everyone:</strong> rely on IRCC's rounds page and WelcomeBC's invitations page for results. Draw dates circulating on social media are not official until they appear there.</li>
      </ul>

